Doubled primes <-> odd cycles <-> Goldbach/Lemoine (oracle-S546)

(1) A000568 from the ODD-cycle Burnside (even cycles -> 0); verify the formula
   n=3: A000568=2  (known 2)  match=True
   n=4: A000568=4  (known 4)  match=True
   n=5: A000568=12  (known 12)  match=True
   n=6: A000568=56  (known 56)  match=True
   n=7: A000568=456  (known 456)  match=True
   n=8: A000568=6880  (known 6880)  match=True

(2) DOUBLED CYCLE = equal odd-cycle pair (l,l) contributes gcd(l,l)=l (MAXIMAL).
    among all-odd partitions of n, the between-cycle exponent term gcd(l_i,l_j):
   n=6:
      (3, 3)         cross-term sum gcd = 3   exponent e=5    <- DOUBLED (equal odd cycles q,q): gcd=q
      (5, 1)         cross-term sum gcd = 1   exponent e=3  
      (3, 1, 1, 1)   cross-term sum gcd = 6   exponent e=7  
   n=10:
      (5, 5)         cross-term sum gcd = 5   exponent e=9    <- DOUBLED (equal odd cycles q,q): gcd=q
      (7, 3)         cross-term sum gcd = 1   exponent e=5  
      (9, 1)         cross-term sum gcd = 1   exponent e=5  
   n=14:
      (7, 7)         cross-term sum gcd = 7   exponent e=13   <- DOUBLED (equal odd cycles q,q): gcd=q
      (11, 3)        cross-term sum gcd = 1   exponent e=7  
      (13, 1)        cross-term sum gcd = 1   exponent e=7  
    => equal odd-cycle pairs (the cycle form of 2q=q+q) give the biggest cross term.

(3) PARITY: even = 2 odd primes (Goldbach); odd = prime + DOUBLED prime (Lemoine p+2q)
   even 10: Goldbach reps p+q = [(3, 7), (5, 5)]...  is 2*prime (diagonal)? True
   even 50: Goldbach reps p+q = [(3, 47), (7, 43), (13, 37), (19, 31)]...  is 2*prime (diagonal)? False
   even 100: Goldbach reps p+q = [(3, 97), (11, 89), (17, 83), (29, 71)]...  is 2*prime (diagonal)? False
   odd  9: Lemoine reps p+2q = [(3, '2*3')]...  (1 reps)
   odd  27: Lemoine reps p+2q = [(17, '2*5'), (13, '2*7'), (5, '2*11')]...  (3 reps)
   odd  99: Lemoine reps p+2q = [(89, '2*5'), (73, '2*13'), (61, '2*19'), (53, '2*23')]...  (9 reps)
   doubled primes 2q (= the diagonal q+q of Goldbach): 4,6,10,14,22,26,34,38,46,...
      [4, 6, 10, 14, 22, 26, 34, 38, 46, 58]

(4) LRC tie: n = 2q (a DOUBLED PRIME) is the rank-one p-adic-tree case (S542)
    n=2q: n/2=q prime -> rank omega(n/2)=1 (single tower), apex speed = q = n/2.
      n=6=2*3: q prime? True; channel rank omega(n/2)=1; apex=n/2=3
      n=10=2*5: q prime? True; channel rank omega(n/2)=1; apex=n/2=5
      n=14=2*7: q prime? True; channel rank omega(n/2)=1; apex=n/2=7
      n=22=2*11: q prime? True; channel rank omega(n/2)=1; apex=n/2=11
      n=26=2*13: q prime? True; channel rank omega(n/2)=1; apex=n/2=13

READING: parity is the parity of the number of ODD parts/cycles: EVEN = 2 odd
primes (Goldbach) = 2 odd cycles; ODD = prime + DOUBLED prime (Lemoine p+2q) = 3 odd
primes with two EQUAL (p,q,q). The DOUBLED prime 2q is (a) the diagonal q+q of
Goldbach, (b) the cycle form of an EQUAL odd-cycle pair, which contributes the
MAXIMAL gcd=q term to the tournament Burnside exponent -- where symmetry
concentrates -- and (c) the rank-one p-adic LRC instances n=2q (cleanest, apex=q).
Doubled primes are the bridge: equal-cycle resonance = diagonal Goldbach = the
single-tower LRC. The '2' is the parity-fixer / the doubling that pairs the cycles.
